    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13070",
      "problem": "A dietician has $25$ patients, $10$ of whom are smokers, and $12$ of whom have diabetes. None of the diabetic patients are smokers. Represent the corresponding sample space using a Venn diagram.",
      "worked_solution": "Let us call $A$ the event \"The patient is a smoker\" and $B$ the event \"The patient has diabetes.\" \n\nSince none of the diabetic patients are smokers, we draw two non-overlapping circles to represent the events $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Since there are $10$ smokers, we write the number $10$ within the circle $A.$\n\n- Since there are $12$ patients with diabetes, we write the number $12$ within the circle $B.$\n\nThere are $25$ patients in total, so the number of remaining patients is \n\n$$\n25 - 10 - 12 = 3.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $3$ remaining patients, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/86855ecd3f1199c9a5c6fbb66ccf08d5b8dd2913d358f52d2c499039ea6fda4d.png)",
